<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The process that reads descriptors on the engine's behalf. It is spawned, never referenced:
    nothing links against it, and it reaches users bundled beside the `dcat` executable rather than
    as a package of its own (it declares no <ReleaseTrain>).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      LatestMajor, and the distinction from the tool's Major matters. Major only rolls up when the
      requested major is ABSENT, so on a machine carrying both .NET 8 and .NET 10 a net8.0 worker
      would bind to 8 and fail to load an analyzer built for 10. LatestMajor always selects the
      highest installed major, so the floor that makes `dcat` installable everywhere (ADR-0017)
      stops deciding what it is able to read.
    -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      Roslyn ships satellite resources for fourteen cultures, and they travel into the tool package
      because the worker travels into it. Not one of them can ever be read: a descriptor's title is
      formatted against the invariant culture on purpose, so that the same upstream release yields
      the same bytes on a maintainer's laptop and on the nightly runner. Keeping them would put ~10 MB
      of translations in a tool that is required never to use a translation.
    -->
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Roslyn lives HERE and only here. Resolving DiagnosticAnalyzer and DiagnosticDescriptor is what
    reading a descriptor is, and reading is all this process does — so the engine, and with it the
    `dcat` process itself, no longer loads Roslyn at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NOT a dependency of this process's own code, which uses no async stream and would compile
    without it. It is deployed because this process is an analyzer HOST, and a host is expected to
    carry identities its guests do not bring.

    An analyzer package ships its own assembly and nothing else — Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers
    declares no <dependencies> at all — because in a real build the compiler is already holding what
    it needs. A shipped analyzer is compiled against netstandard2.0, where IAsyncEnumerable<T> and
    IAsyncDisposable live in Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces rather than in the runtime, so its
    reference table names that assembly. The runtime this worker runs on HAS both types; what it
    does not have is that name, and this is the one netstandard-era compat facade the shared
    framework does not ship (System.Threading.Tasks.Extensions and
    System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe are both in it, which is why neither ever surfaced).
    Deploying the package resolves the name onto types that were there all along.

    The reason this worker meets the problem at all, where the compiler does not, is that it
    materialises EVERY type in the assembly: it selects analyzers by base type, so it calls
    Assembly.GetTypes() rather than reading metadata for the types the [DiagnosticAnalyzer] attribute
    names. That reaches the code fixes and the internal helpers too — types the compiler never loads
    and this process never needs. Nothing here is lost by loading them, but a type that fails to load
    has no name to ask about, so the run cannot tell a code fix from an analyzer and refuses the
    whole read (ADR-0024). Selecting types the way the compiler selects them would be the deeper
    answer and a change of contract; supplying the identity is the correct answer either way, since a
    host that cannot resolve what its guests reference is a host that refuses valid packages.

    Measured on the packages it unblocks: Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.CodeStyle (121 rules),
    Meziantou.Analyzer (210) and Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers (23) are all refused
    without it and read completely with it, and their descriptor counts are identical either way —
    the types that were being dropped never declared a rule.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    LINKED SOURCE, not a project reference, and the distinction is load-bearing.

    The worker is launched with `dotnet exec` against a target's own deps.json whenever the assemblies
    it is given came with a dependency graph, so that an analyzer compiled against another Roslyn
    resolves its own. That switch REPLACES the worker's dependency graph rather than adding to it —
    so any managed dependency of the worker's own becomes unresolvable, because no analyzer's
    deps.json will ever mention CatalogGen. Measured, not assumed: with a ProjectReference the
    worker died with "File name: 'CatalogGen, Version=1.0.0.0'" on the first target that had a
    graph.
    (This is where the sibling repository's worker differs rather than disagrees: the library ITS
    worker needs is one the target also references, so the target's graph carries it.)

    Compiling the two files in means the worker's only managed dependency is Roslyn, which the
    target's graph either supplies or the probing path does. Naming has to be the same code on both
    sides of the wire — a title normalised one way and read back another would report every rule as
    retitled on every run — and sharing the source is what guarantees that; the two copies cannot
    drift because there is only one file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\CatalogGen\Naming.cs" Link="Naming.cs" />
    <Compile Include="..\CatalogGen\DescriptorReadContract.cs" Link="DescriptorReadContract.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
